12,4.5]},{"tree":[-50.674,-40.893,12,4.5]},{"tree":[31.424,-58.005,12,4.5]},{"tree":[38.265,-39.211,12,4.5]},{"tree":[17.084,-15.539,12,4.5]},{"tree":[47.002,37.422,12,4.5]},{"tree":[-70.34,-77.37,17,4.5]}],"notes":"Fenway Court (Willard T. Sears for Isabella Stewart Gardner, 1903): a four-storey Venetian palazzo turned inside out, its outer walls plain buff stucco with a few Venetian windows and balconies, round the glass-roofed garden court; and Renzo Piano's wing (2012) behind it, a glass ground storey with volumes clad in pre-patinated copper above (Calderwood Hall's cube, the exhibition gallery, the glass 'living room'), the greenhouses, and the glass link. Footprints and the slight skew from the OpenStreetMap ground; description from general knowledge of the building. Refined 29 Sep 2026 (no photographs reachable): the palazzo's stucco made a warmer buff (it read white), its flat roofs grey behind the parapet, and more of its irregular windows put in on the second and fourth storeys; the garden court's glass roof pitched, as it is, rather than a block of glass; the Piano wing's pre-patinated copper drawn in the new copper material (it was the pale paintbrick, and read white). The Back Bay Fens across the Fenway are just off the plate. Guessed: window counts and positions, the stair tower, the copper's exact shade."}